\DOC NONSELFMODIFYING_STATE_UPDATE_TAC

\TYPE {NONSELFMODIFYING_STATE_UPDATE_TAC : thm -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Replaces an assumption about one machine state with the corresponding
assumption about a successor state, using a supplied theorem and the
state-update equation occurring in the goal's conclusion.

\DESCRIBE
The argument {bth : thm} is a theorem of the form "if a property holds of a
state at some address, then it holds of any state whose relevant memory region
reads the same"; its conclusion determines which assumption is transferred and
to which state. The tactic locates the assumption matching the premise of {th}
for a state {t}, discharges it, and adds in its place the same assumption with
{t} replaced by the successor state {u} named in the goal's conclusion — the
state given by an equation of the form "a nest of state updates applied to {t}
equals {u}". The conclusion of the goal is left unchanged, and exactly one
assumption is modified. The replacement assumption is appended at the end of
the assumption list, so the remaining assumptions keep their relative order but
shift index.
{
       A, P t ?- s
     ================  NONSELFMODIFYING_STATE_UPDATE_TAC bth
       A, P u ?- s
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces the behaviour is this same single-goal,
one-assumption-rewritten transformation, on goals whose conclusion is either an
implication from a state-update equation or such an implication with a further
{eventually} consequent.

\ENDDOC
